# Generate the real file next to this one:
#
#   docker run --rm httpd:alpine htpasswd -nbB -C 12 matt 'a-long-random-password' > config/.htpasswd
#
# -B is bcrypt. Do NOT use -n without -B; nginx accepts the weaker formats and
# this file is what stands between the internet and your campaign records.
#
# -C 12 IS NOT OPTIONAL, and leaving it off is the quiet mistake this line
# exists to stop. `htpasswd -B` on its own defaults to cost 5, which is about
# 128 times cheaper to attack than cost 12 — a hash somebody has taken a copy
# of falls to a wordlist far faster than the "it's bcrypt" label suggests. This
# install ran at cost 5 for months because the advice above used to stop at -B.
#
# Cost 12 takes roughly a quarter-second to verify on a Pi, which is invisible
# on a page you open a few times a day and expensive for anyone guessing.
#
# The password itself should be long and random rather than memorable. It is
# typed once and then remembered by the browser, so there is nothing to gain by
# making it something a person could invent:
#
#   tr -dc 'A-HJ-NP-Za-km-z2-9' < /dev/urandom | head -c 32
#
# config/.htpasswd is gitignored.

# WHAT THIS IS AND IS NOT
#
# It is a coarse outer fence, and it is one shared password for one user. It
# cannot say WHO came in, cannot be revoked for one person, and leaves no trail
# of who opened what. Anyone holding it is, as far as this file is concerned,
# the same visitor.
#
# The real identity boundary is DASHBOARD_REQUIRE_LOGIN in pi-service/.env.
# With that on, getting past this password buys nothing on its own: a request
# with no Discord session is level `none`, every capability is false, /status
# comes back with no campaigns in it and every action answers 403. Turn it on.
# This file is the fence around the field, not the lock on the door.

# One more thing, and it is the one that bites: nginx's WORKERS run as uid 101
# inside the container, and `htpasswd >` writes 600 owned by you. nginx then
# cannot read its own password file, and every request that should have got a
# password prompt gets 500 instead — which looks like the site being broken
# rather than like a permissions problem, and only shows up for people the
# allow-list does not cover, so it can sit there unnoticed for months.
#
#   chmod 644 config/.htpasswd
#
# nginx re-reads this file on every request, so changing the password needs no
# restart. Check it took before you rely on it, from an address the allow-list
# does not cover:
#
#   curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' -u matt:the-new-one \
#     -H 'CF-Connecting-IP: 8.8.8.8' https://your-host/app/
#
# It is a bcrypt hash, not a password.
